1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,434
As you listen to this sentence, about 65 billion neutrinos from the Sun pass every second through the nail of your

2
00:00:06,434 --> 00:00:07,653
little finger.

3
00:00:08,003 --> 00:00:09,562
You don't feel any of them.

4
00:00:09,912 --> 00:00:11,620
None of them even touches you.

5
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:16,686
So how do you catch a particle that hardly ever hits anything?

6
00:00:17,036 --> 00:00:23,894
The 2026 Nobel Prize in Physics has a surprising answer: by looking two kilometres down into the ice at the South Pole.

7
00:00:24,694 --> 00:00:27,362
Hello, and welcome to the BosNRoll channel.

8
00:00:27,712 --> 00:00:33,613
Today we're covering this year's Nobel Prize in Physics, its laureate Francis Halzen, and the shyest particle in the universe:

9
00:00:33,613 --> 00:00:34,974
the neutrino.

10
00:00:35,324 --> 00:00:38,141
All information comes from the Nobel Committee's official documents.

11
00:00:38,491 --> 00:00:44,365
First the 5W1H summary, then the full story, and at the end, as always, our logic check.

12
00:00:45,165 --> 00:00:48,404
Who? Belgian-born physicist Francis Halzen.

13
00:00:48,754 --> 00:00:57,448
He was born in 1944 in Tienen, Belgium, and completed his PhD at the Catholic University of Leuven in 1969.

14
00:00:57,798 --> 00:01:01,387
For many years he has been a professor at the University of Wisconsin–Madison in the United States.

15
00:01:02,187 --> 00:01:08,602
What? The Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences awarded the prize for his decisive contributions to the IceCube Neutrino Observatory and

16
00:01:08,602 --> 00:01:12,267
the discovery of high-energy neutrinos from space.

17
00:01:12,617 --> 00:01:15,064
The prize amount is 12 million Swedish kronor.

18
00:01:15,864 --> 00:01:18,306
Where? The discovery was made at the South Pole.

19
00:01:18,656 --> 00:01:25,501
Near the Amundsen–Scott research station, between 1,450 and 2,450 metres deep in the ice,

20
00:01:25,501 --> 00:01:27,855
sits a detector one cubic kilometre in size.

21
00:01:28,655 --> 00:01:32,013
When? The prize was announced on 6 October 2026.

22
00:01:32,363 --> 00:01:38,046
The idea itself is much older: Halzen first proposed it in 1988.

23
00:01:38,396 --> 00:01:41,160
So this is a story almost forty years in the making.

24
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:46,586
How? 5,160 light sensors were placed deep in the ice.

25
00:01:46,936 --> 00:01:53,828
On the rare occasions a neutrino hits an atomic nucleus in the ice, it creates a tiny flash of blue light.

26
00:01:54,178 --> 00:01:56,316
The sensors catch exactly that light.

27
00:01:57,116 --> 00:02:03,352
Why? Because neutrinos reach us from the most violent events in the universe without changing direction or losing

28
00:02:03,352 --> 00:02:03,882
energy.

29
00:02:04,232 --> 00:02:07,448
They carry information that can't be obtained in any other way.

30
00:02:08,248 --> 00:02:10,270
Chapter one: what is a neutrino?

31
00:02:11,070 --> 00:02:17,991
The story begins in 1930. To balance the energy in radioactive decays, physicist Wolfgang Pauli

32
00:02:17,991 --> 00:02:21,640
proposed that there must be a new, invisible particle.

33
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:26,380
Its existence was only proven in 1956.

34
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:33,023
Clyde Cowan and Frederick Reines first caught the neutrino with an experiment set up next to a nuclear reactor.

35
00:02:33,823 --> 00:02:38,105
The neutrino has no electric charge, and its mass is almost zero.

36
00:02:38,455 --> 00:02:41,100
That's why it very rarely interacts with matter.

37
00:02:41,450 --> 00:02:46,875
It can enter one side of the Earth and come out the other, and it passes through our bodies unnoticed.

38
00:02:47,675 --> 00:02:52,925
Today we know there are three kinds of neutrinos: electron, muon and tau neutrinos.

39
00:02:53,275 --> 00:02:57,284
What's more, neutrinos can change from one kind into another as they travel.

40
00:02:57,634 --> 00:03:02,925
That discovery won the 2015 Nobel Prize in Physics and showed that neutrinos have mass.

41
00:03:03,725 --> 00:03:05,829
Neutrinos had been caught from space before.

42
00:03:06,179 --> 00:03:10,831
In the 1960s, Raymond Davis observed neutrinos from the Sun.

43
00:03:11,181 --> 00:03:18,604
In 1987, the Kamiokande detector in Japan caught a burst of neutrinos from a star that exploded in our neighbouring galaxy,

44
00:03:18,604 --> 00:03:20,941
the Large Magellanic Cloud.

45
00:03:21,291 --> 00:03:23,709
This work was awarded the Nobel Prize in 2002.

46
00:03:24,509 --> 00:03:27,179
But those neutrinos had relatively low energies.

47
00:03:27,529 --> 00:03:33,236
What Halzen was after was something else entirely: neutrinos about a billion times more energetic than solar neutrinos,

48
00:03:33,236 --> 00:03:35,707
coming from the depths of the universe.

49
00:03:36,507 --> 00:03:39,935
Chapter two: the universe's particle accelerators.

50
00:03:40,735 --> 00:03:43,571
Particles constantly rain down on Earth from space.

51
00:03:43,921 --> 00:03:48,768
They're called cosmic rays, and they were discovered by Victor Hess in 1912.

52
00:03:49,118 --> 00:03:53,108
Most of them are protons, the bare nuclei of hydrogen atoms.

53
00:03:53,908 --> 00:03:58,003
Some of these particles have mind-boggling energies.

54
00:03:58,353 --> 00:04:04,321
According to the Nobel Committee, natural accelerators in the universe fire out particles with energies up to a million

55
00:04:04,321 --> 00:04:06,950
times higher than we can reach in laboratories on Earth.

56
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:13,067
The problem is that we still don't fully know what these accelerators are or where they are.

57
00:04:13,867 --> 00:04:15,909
Protons don't show us the way.

58
00:04:16,259 --> 00:04:20,244
Because they're electrically charged, their paths bend in magnetic fields in space.

59
00:04:20,594 --> 00:04:24,224
By the time they reach Earth, we can no longer tell where they came from.

60
00:04:25,024 --> 00:04:28,255
Gamma rays are a better messenger, because they travel in straight lines.

61
00:04:28,605 --> 00:04:33,608
But they can weaken along the way by interacting with matter and light, and they can't show us what lies behind thick clouds of dust and

62
00:04:33,608 --> 00:04:34,424
gas.

63
00:04:35,224 --> 00:04:38,939
Neutrinos, on the other hand, are neither caught by magnetic fields nor absorbed along the way.

64
00:04:39,289 --> 00:04:41,659
They travel in a straight line from where they were born.

65
00:04:42,009 --> 00:04:47,227
So if you find the direction a neutrino came from, you've found the address of the cosmic accelerator.

66
00:04:48,027 --> 00:04:49,892
So how are these neutrinos made?

67
00:04:50,242 --> 00:04:56,491
Accelerated protons hit surrounding gas or intense light, producing short-lived particles called

68
00:04:56,491 --> 00:04:56,945
pions.

69
00:04:57,295 --> 00:05:01,578
Neutral pions decay into gamma rays, while charged pions decay into neutrinos.

70
00:05:01,928 --> 00:05:05,059
So the neutrino is the fingerprint of the process that accelerates the protons.

71
00:05:05,859 --> 00:05:12,608
Candidate sources include active galaxies, where matter falls into a supermassive black hole at the centre and giant jets

72
00:05:12,608 --> 00:05:13,528
are launched into space.

73
00:05:13,878 --> 00:05:16,902
Exploding stars and gamma-ray bursts are also on the list.

74
00:05:17,702 --> 00:05:20,460
Chapter three: the idea of a telescope made of ice.

75
00:05:21,260 --> 00:05:27,111
Halzen grew up in Belgium and was drawn to particle physics by the progress of the 1960s.

76
00:05:27,461 --> 00:05:31,800
Shortly after his PhD, he moved to the University of Wisconsin–Madison.

77
00:05:32,150 --> 00:05:38,571
Over time, he became interested in what particles from space could teach both particle physics and

78
00:05:38,571 --> 00:05:39,842
astrophysics.

79
00:05:40,642 --> 00:05:45,237
In the autumn of 1987, Halzen gave a talk at the University of Kansas.

80
00:05:45,587 --> 00:05:50,457
A glaciologist in the audience told him that Soviet researchers planned to use the Antarctic ice

81
00:05:50,457 --> 00:05:55,100
to capture radio waves emitted by neutrino collisions.

82
00:05:55,450 --> 00:06:02,010
Halzen and his colleagues calculated that this method would not be sensitive enough for the neutrinos they were looking for.

83
00:06:02,810 --> 00:06:06,743
Instead, he had another idea: not radio waves, but light.

84
00:06:07,093 --> 00:06:12,486
Around 1960, some physicists had proposed that water could be used to catch neutrinos.

85
00:06:12,836 --> 00:06:18,920
On the rare occasions a neutrino hits an atomic nucleus, a charged particle is created that travels in the same direction,

86
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:21,630
leaving a trail of blue light behind it.

87
00:06:21,980 --> 00:06:23,915
This is called Cherenkov light.

88
00:06:24,715 --> 00:06:31,564
It forms when a charged particle moves through water or ice faster than light travels in that medium.

89
00:06:31,914 --> 00:06:38,302
It's like the shock wave behind a plane that breaks the sound barrier, only with light instead of sound.

90
00:06:38,652 --> 00:06:43,655
The angle of the light and the order in which it reaches the sensors reveal the direction the particle came from.

91
00:06:44,455 --> 00:06:48,279
What set Halzen apart was using the natural ice of the South Pole instead of water.

92
00:06:48,629 --> 00:06:54,129
In his own words: had he known back then what was known about the optical properties of natural ice,

93
00:06:54,129 --> 00:06:56,487
he would probably have given up on the idea too.

94
00:06:57,287 --> 00:07:03,661
Halzen wrote to John Learned, spokesperson of the DUMAND project, which was trying to build a similar detector deep in the ocean

95
00:07:03,661 --> 00:07:05,422
off Hawaii.

96
00:07:05,771 --> 00:07:13,065
The two first presented the idea of a neutrino observatory in the South Pole ice in 1988, at a conference in Poland.

97
00:07:13,865 --> 00:07:16,108
The South Pole had many advantages.

98
00:07:16,458 --> 00:07:19,102
There was already a research station there.

99
00:07:19,452 --> 00:07:25,975
Deep down, the ice is pitch dark; there are no light-emitting creatures like in the ocean, and little radioactive material.

100
00:07:26,325 --> 00:07:28,973
The region is geologically calm, with no earthquakes.

101
00:07:29,323 --> 00:07:35,663
The downside is the cold: all field work has to fit into the short Antarctic summer between November and

102
00:07:35,663 --> 00:07:36,155
February.

103
00:07:36,955 --> 00:07:39,024
Chapter four: drilling into the ice.

104
00:07:39,824 --> 00:07:41,423
First, a test was needed.

105
00:07:41,773 --> 00:07:49,216
In 1991, sensors lowered into boreholes in the Greenland ice sheet showed that the ice was clear enough

106
00:07:49,216 --> 00:07:50,516
to carry this light.

107
00:07:51,316 --> 00:07:55,316
Then AMANDA, the predecessor of IceCube, was built at the South Pole.

108
00:07:55,666 --> 00:08:00,677
Kilometre-deep holes were melted open, using a method learned from glaciologists:

109
00:08:00,677 --> 00:08:04,389
a giant shower head spraying hot water.

110
00:08:04,739 --> 00:08:12,301
Halzen recalls sitting at the dinner table on Christmas Eve 1993, laptop on his lap, waiting for news that the first sensor cable

111
00:08:12,301 --> 00:08:14,432
had been lowered into the ice.

112
00:08:15,232 --> 00:08:17,102
The first results were disappointing.

113
00:08:17,452 --> 00:08:22,101
The upper layers of ice were full of tiny air bubbles that scattered the light in every direction.

114
00:08:22,451 --> 00:08:24,945
But deeper down, the ice was extraordinarily clear.

115
00:08:25,295 --> 00:08:30,107
Below 1,400 metres, light could travel 300 metres before being absorbed.

116
00:08:30,457 --> 00:08:32,217
Much further than anyone expected.

117
00:08:33,017 --> 00:08:37,754
AMANDA worked, but it wasn't big enough to catch cosmic neutrinos.

118
00:08:38,104 --> 00:08:41,262
What was needed was a cubic kilometre of ice.

119
00:08:41,612 --> 00:08:49,109
The IceCube proposal, with Halzen as principal investigator, was submitted in 1999, funding was approved in 2002,

120
00:08:49,109 --> 00:08:50,943
and construction began in 2004.

121
00:08:51,743 --> 00:08:56,948
Between 2005 and 2010, 86 cables were lowered into holes in the ice.

122
00:08:57,298 --> 00:09:03,683
Each cable carries 60 sensors, arranged between 1,450 and 2,450 metres deep.

123
00:09:04,033 --> 00:09:07,056
The cables are 125 metres apart.

124
00:09:07,406 --> 00:09:11,494
In total, 5,160 sensors watch a full cubic kilometre of ice.

125
00:09:12,294 --> 00:09:15,564
On the surface, there is also an array of detectors called IceTop.

126
00:09:15,914 --> 00:09:21,492
It measures the particle showers created when cosmic rays hit the atmosphere, and gives the detector below

127
00:09:21,492 --> 00:09:25,452
a kind of warning signal to filter out noise from above.

128
00:09:26,252 --> 00:09:29,185
That's an ice mass of roughly one billion tonnes.

129
00:09:29,535 --> 00:09:36,877
Halzen compares these sensors to a light bulb working in reverse: instead of emitting light, they capture it and turn it into an electrical

130
00:09:36,877 --> 00:09:38,262
signal.

131
00:09:39,062 --> 00:09:41,012
IceCube sees two kinds of events.

132
00:09:41,362 --> 00:09:46,809
Muon neutrinos leave a long track in the ice, from which their direction can be measured very precisely.

133
00:09:47,159 --> 00:09:50,745
Other collisions create a ball-shaped burst of light.

134
00:09:51,095 --> 00:09:54,404
Their direction is less certain, but their energy is measured very well.

135
00:09:55,204 --> 00:09:57,879
The hardest part is finding a needle in a haystack.

136
00:09:58,229 --> 00:10:03,172
Every day, IceCube records more than a hundred million particles coming from the atmosphere.

137
00:10:03,522 --> 00:10:08,313
It sees about 100,000 neutrinos a year, and most of these are also produced in the atmosphere.

138
00:10:08,663 --> 00:10:11,971
Only about 100 a year are expected to come from space.

139
00:10:12,771 --> 00:10:16,175
The trick is to use the Earth itself as a shield.

140
00:10:16,525 --> 00:10:21,668
If you select tracks coming from the northern hemisphere, that is, from below after passing through the planet,

141
00:10:21,668 --> 00:10:25,118
you remove almost all the muons from the atmosphere.

142
00:10:25,468 --> 00:10:28,348
Because only neutrinos can pass through the Earth.

143
00:10:29,148 --> 00:10:31,068
Chapter five: the discovery.

144
00:10:31,868 --> 00:10:41,335
In 2013, two extraordinary events were found in two years of data: two neutrinos with energies of 1.04 and 1.14

145
00:10:41,335 --> 00:10:42,378
petaelectronvolts.

146
00:10:42,728 --> 00:10:49,040
Interestingly, the team found them by chance while searching for other, even higher-energy neutrinos.

147
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:53,735
The same data revealed 28 more high-energy neutrinos.

148
00:10:54,085 --> 00:11:01,558
In 2014, the team rejected the possibility that these neutrinos came only from the atmosphere at a level of 5.7 sigma.

149
00:11:01,908 --> 00:11:05,938
In physics, 5 sigma is the accepted threshold for claiming a discovery.

150
00:11:06,288 --> 00:11:09,768
High-energy neutrinos from space were now real.

151
00:11:10,568 --> 00:11:17,081
So what does sigma mean? Roughly, it describes how unlikely it is that the signal you see appeared by

152
00:11:17,081 --> 00:11:17,684
chance.

153
00:11:18,034 --> 00:11:23,903
5 sigma means the odds of the result being a fluke are about one in 3.5 million.

154
00:11:24,253 --> 00:11:28,601
That's why physicists only use the word discovery after crossing this threshold.

155
00:11:29,401 --> 00:11:35,663
The most energetic neutrino IceCube has published so far was recorded on 31 March 2019.

156
00:11:36,013 --> 00:11:39,213
Its energy was about 11.4 petaelectronvolts.

157
00:11:40,013 --> 00:11:43,833
On 22 September 2017, there was an exciting moment.

158
00:11:44,183 --> 00:11:48,515
IceCube caught a neutrino of about 290 teraelectronvolts.

159
00:11:48,865 --> 00:11:53,345
Its direction matched almost exactly the position of a blazar called TXS.

160
00:11:53,695 --> 00:11:57,266
A blazar is an active galaxy whose jet points directly at us.

161
00:11:57,616 --> 00:12:02,320
Within a minute, an alert went out to the world and telescopes turned in that direction.

162
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:05,553
In 2022, another step was taken.

163
00:12:05,903 --> 00:12:14,638
IceCube announced that 79 neutrinos had come from the direction of NGC 1068, an active galaxy 46 million light-years away.

164
00:12:14,988 --> 00:12:19,926
The black hole at the centre of this galaxy is wrapped in a thick layer of gas and dust.

165
00:12:20,276 --> 00:12:24,809
So with neutrinos, we may be looking into a region our telescopes cannot see.

166
00:12:25,609 --> 00:12:31,680
In 2023, high-energy neutrinos from our own galaxy, the Milky Way, were shown for the first time.

167
00:12:32,030 --> 00:12:36,463
With an analysis published this year based on 12 years of data, the result crossed the 5 sigma threshold.

168
00:12:36,813 --> 00:12:41,875
That made the Milky Way the first high-energy neutrino source to pass the discovery threshold.

169
00:12:42,675 --> 00:12:47,398
There's more evidence. In 2024, seven high-energy tau neutrinos were published.

170
00:12:47,748 --> 00:12:54,284
Tau neutrinos with such high energies can't be produced in the atmosphere, so these must have come from space.

171
00:12:54,634 --> 00:12:59,230
The possibility that no tau neutrinos come from space was also rejected at the 5 sigma level.

172
00:13:00,030 --> 00:13:05,529
In the words of Mark Pearce, Chair of the Nobel Committee for Physics: Halzen's tenacity and scientific vision

173
00:13:05,529 --> 00:13:07,886
paved the way for a new kind of astronomy.

174
00:13:08,686 --> 00:13:12,024
Now for our channel's signature: the logic check.

175
00:13:12,824 --> 00:13:20,284
Question one. IceCube is run by an international collaboration of about 450 people at 58 institutions in 14

176
00:13:20,284 --> 00:13:21,144
countries.

177
00:13:21,494 --> 00:13:23,373
Yet the prize went to a single person.

178
00:13:23,723 --> 00:13:30,208
Is that fair? The committee's reasoning is that Halzen came up with the idea and has been principal investigator from the very start.

179
00:13:30,558 --> 00:13:37,405
Nobel rules limit each prize to at most three people, so this debate is unavoidable for big team projects.

180
00:13:37,755 --> 00:13:43,197
The committee doesn't hide it either, and specifically stresses that Halzen led an international team.

181
00:13:43,997 --> 00:13:46,983
Question two: have the cosmic accelerators been found?

182
00:13:47,333 --> 00:13:49,253
No, and that distinction matters.

183
00:13:49,603 --> 00:13:54,355
The prize was awarded for discovering that high-energy neutrinos from space exist.

184
00:13:54,705 --> 00:13:58,265
Where these neutrinos come from is still largely unknown.

185
00:13:58,615 --> 00:14:04,631
Even the committee's own text says the evidence is not yet strong enough to confirm NGC 1068 as a

186
00:14:04,631 --> 00:14:05,763
source.

187
00:14:06,113 --> 00:14:10,487
So if you see headlines saying the secret of the universe has been solved, be a little cautious.

188
00:14:11,287 --> 00:14:14,294
Our verdict: the prize was awarded for a solid discovery.

189
00:14:14,644 --> 00:14:19,250
But the real question, where exactly these neutrinos come from, is still open.

190
00:14:20,050 --> 00:14:21,894
Final chapter: what's next?

191
00:14:22,694 --> 00:14:24,941
Neutrino astronomy is only just beginning.

192
00:14:25,291 --> 00:14:30,030
At the South Pole, an eight-cubic-kilometre IceCube-Gen2 is being planned.

193
00:14:30,380 --> 00:14:37,797
KM3NeT in the Mediterranean, Baikal-GVD in Lake Baikal in Siberia, P-ONE off the coast of Canada, and in the South China Sea

194
00:14:37,797 --> 00:14:41,162
TRIDENT are building similar telescopes in water.

195
00:14:41,962 --> 00:14:48,083
The goal is to combine neutrinos with gamma rays, cosmic rays and perhaps gravitational waves to build a complete picture of the universe's most

196
00:14:48,083 --> 00:14:50,555
violent events.

197
00:14:50,905 --> 00:14:52,915
This is called multi-messenger astronomy.

198
00:14:53,715 --> 00:14:59,054
An idea first presented at a conference in 1988, whose first attempts ended in disappointment,

199
00:14:59,054 --> 00:15:05,837
has become an observatory at the South Pole that runs without interruption more than ninety-nine percent of the time.

200
00:15:06,187 --> 00:15:09,086
And every day, it keeps catching new neutrinos.

201
00:15:09,886 --> 00:15:16,214
That's all for today. All sources are in the description; I especially recommend reading the Nobel Committee's scientific

202
00:15:16,214 --> 00:15:16,756
report.

203
00:15:17,106 --> 00:15:20,786
Do you think the Nobel Prize should go to a single person in big team projects?

204
00:15:21,136 --> 00:15:25,894
Let's discuss in the comments. Don't forget to subscribe to the BosNRoll channel. See you next time.
